Show how to upgrade, and optionally whether a newer release exists.

Without `--check` this contacts nothing: it reads the path of the running binary, and the files pip leaves beside it, to work out which channel installed it — cargo, Homebrew, Scoop, winget, npm, pip or the install script — and prints that channel's upgrade command. Where the install script and pip share a folder and nothing tells them apart, it prints both; where it cannot tell at all, every channel's.

⚠️ evnx never replaces its own binary. Each package manager owns the file it installed, and a self-updating binary fights it — Homebrew and winget would then report a version they did not install.

`--check` trusts evnx's bundled root certificates plus any in the PEM bundle named by SSL_CERT_FILE or the directory named by SSL_CERT_DIR. Behind a TLS-inspecting proxy, set SSL_CERT_FILE to your organisation's CA bundle.

Usage: evnx update [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --check
          Ask api.github.com for the latest release.
          
          The only network request this command makes, and it is made only when you pass this flag. Nothing is sent but the request itself.
          
          Needs a build with network support (`cloud`, `migrate` or `net`); a bare `cargo install evnx` has none, and says so.

      --list
          List every copy of evnx on this machine, and which one runs.
          
          Looks on PATH, in order, then in the folders installers use (cargo, npm, pipx, Homebrew, ~/.local/bin). Versions are read from what the installer left; a copy with none is run with `--version`, for at most two seconds, and the output says so. No network. Exits 0 however many copies there are; `--json` gives `count` for a script to decide.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Output:
      --format <FORMAT>
          With `--check` or `--list`: the answer as JSON on stdout.
          
          `--check`: `schema` (1), `installed`, `latest`, `status` (`current`, `behind` or `ahead`), `channel`, `command` (null when evnx cannot name one), `release_url` and `other_copies`. `--list`: `schema` (1), `count` and `copies`. The exit code is the same as without it.
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json]

      --json
          With `--check` or `--list`: the answer as JSON on stdout. Same as --format json.

Without --check the exit code is 0, however many copies --list finds. With
--check, 1 means a newer release exists, and 2 that it could not tell.

More than one evnx installed? `evnx update --list` shows each copy, its
version, which one runs, and how to remove the rest. Only --check uses the
network.

-q prints only the upgrade command; with --check, the status line and the command.

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    1  Ran, and found something: findings, problems, a difference, not in sync
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/update
